::PC_U06_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U06_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
For f(x)=x², what is the average rate of change from x=1 to x=4
{
~B. 3#Incorrect. 3 is the input change only. Average rate of change divides output change by input change.
~C. 15#Incorrect. 15 is the output change before dividing by the input change.
~D. 16#Incorrect. 16 is f(4), not the average rate over the interval.
=A. 5#Correct. f(4)=16 and f(1)=1, so the output change is 15 and the input change is 3. The average rate is 5.}

::PC_U06_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U06_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
For f(x)=2x+3, what is (f(x+h)-f(x))/h after simplification
{
~A. h#Incorrect. h is the input change, not the simplified quotient.
~C. 2h#Incorrect. 2h is the numerator before dividing by h.
~D. 2x+3#Incorrect. That is f(x), not the simplified difference quotient.
=B. 2#Correct. f(x+h)=2x+2h+3, so the numerator is 2h and 2h/h=2.}

::PC_U06_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U06_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
If f(x) approaches 6 as x approaches 2, which statement correctly describes the behavior
{
~B. x equals 6 for every output#Incorrect. This does not describe nearby function behavior.
~C. x approaches 6 as f(x) approaches 2#Incorrect. This reverses input and output roles.
=A. f(x) approaches 6 as x approaches 2#Correct. This identifies the input value 2 and the output value 6 in the proper roles.
~D. f(x) equals 2 as x approaches 6#Incorrect. This swaps the values and changes approach language into an exact equality.}

::PC_U06_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U06_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
A graph gets closer and closer to the horizontal line y=3 on the right side. What is the best description
{
~A. The graph has x\=3 as a vertical asymptote#Incorrect. The line described is y=3, so it is horizontal, not vertical.
~C. The graph must cross y\=3 at every point#Incorrect. Approaching a line does not mean crossing it at every input.
~D. The graph has no end behavior#Incorrect. The description is specifically about behavior as x moves right.
=B. The graph approaches the horizontal asymptote y\=3#Correct. A graph getting closer to y=3 shows horizontal asymptote behavior.}

::PC_U06_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U06_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.6
Question:
The left-side outputs approach 2 and the right-side outputs approach 8 as x approaches 0. What should you conclude
{
~A. f(x) approaches 10 as x approaches 0#Incorrect. Adding the two approach values is not how function behavior near a point is described.
~B. f(x) approaches 8 from both sides#Incorrect. Only the right-side outputs approach 8; the left-side outputs approach 2.
=C. There is no single shared approach value from both sides#Correct. The two sides approach different output values, so there is no one shared approach value.
~D. x approaches 2 and 8#Incorrect. The input approaches 0. The values 2 and 8 are output behaviors.}
